INSERT INTO stories (id, date, month, summary_en, html_en, source_url, published) VALUES ('2026-02-05-sweden-special-investigator-appointed', '2026-02-05', '2026-02', 'Sweden''s Ministry of Finance has appointed a special investigator (Dir. 2026:9) to determine how the EU''s ViDA framework should be implemented in Swedish law, and separately to assess whether e-invoicing and digital reporting should be introduced for purely domestic transactions — with findings due by 30 November 2027.', '<h3>🇸🇪 Sweden appoints a special investigator to assess a domestic e-invoicing mandate</h3>
<p>Sweden''s Ministry of Finance formally appointed a special investigator (särskild utredare) under government directive Dir. 2026:9 to examine two genuinely separate questions. The first is essentially mandatory: identifying the legislative changes needed to implement the EU''s ViDA framework for cross-border B2B digital reporting, which Sweden must transpose regardless of its own domestic preferences. The second is genuinely open — whether e-invoicing and digital reporting should also be introduced for purely domestic Swedish transactions, and if so, how far such a system should reach. Findings are due no later than 30 November 2027, timed to allow implementation ahead of the EU''s own 1 July 2030 cross-border deadline.</p>
<p>Worth understanding Sweden''s starting position clearly: unlike Norway, which has already legislated a firm 2027 domestic B2B mandate, or Denmark''s established digital bookkeeping model, Sweden has historically shown genuine reluctance to legislate any domestic e-invoicing mandate at all. This investigation follows a formal request (hemställan) submitted in 2023 by Digg, Bolagsverket, and Skatteverket jointly, and a further request from the Swedish Business and Industry Tax Delegation in July 2025 — meaning the pressure for this review came substantially from Swedish institutions and industry themselves, not purely from Brussels.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> Sweden''s domestic B2B e-invoicing position remains genuinely undecided until at least late 2027 — don''t assume Sweden will follow Norway''s or Denmark''s more decisive timelines just because they''re Nordic neighbours. What is already certain is the EU cross-border requirement from 2030, so cross-border ViDA compliance planning shouldn''t wait on this domestic investigation''s outcome, even though the domestic question remains genuinely open.</p>', 'https://www.skatteverket.se', 1);
